 from arc_utils import Color
 
-
 # Design analysis for the D14 variant
-# -----------------------------------
-# a) Concrete behavior and trace
-#    - The canvas still contains two large foreground regions on black background.
-#    - Black cells inside a region are ordinary dents: they still become 3x3 patches
-#      of the opposite foreground color with a teal center.
-#    - The change is at region boundaries: a one-cell foreground protrusion is no
-#      longer "cleaned away". Instead, it becomes another trigger center and receives
-#      the same output treatment as a dent: stamp a 3x3 patch and paint the center teal.
-#    - Small trace:
-#      original rule: host-color cell -> wrong opposite-color boundary speck -> cleanup
-#      repaints it back to host -> nothing special remains in output.
-#      variant rule: the same wrong boundary speck is first recognized as a protrusion,
-#      the clean base restores the host cell underneath, then a 3x3 patch of the
-#      protruding color is stamped and the center becomes teal.
-#      Difference: the boundary cell now creates a visible patch instead of disappearing.
+# a) Concretization and behavior trace
+#    - The canvas still contains two large rectangular foreground regions on black.
+#    - Black dents remain true holes: a host-colored cell is replaced by black in the input.
+#    - The new object type is a one-cell attached protrusion at the boundary between the
+#      two foreground regions: a single source-colored cell is painted one step into the
+#      opposite region while staying 4-connected to its source region.
+#    - Original flow on such a cell:
+#      clean_foreground() rewrites it back to the surrounding host color, so it vanishes.
+#    - Variant flow:
+#      the same cleanup still reconstructs the clean baseline, but the rewritten cell is
+#      recorded as a trigger center. Its 3x3 neighborhood is then stamped with the source
+#      color and the center becomes teal, just like a dent produces a 3x3 patch + teal.
+#    - Small example: if a blue cell protrudes one step into a red block, the old solver
+#      would restore that cell to red. The new solver still restores the baseline to red,
+#      but then stamps a blue 3x3 patch centered there and colors the center teal.
+#
 # b) Complexity calibration
-#    - Only one new trigger family is added: single-cell protrusions.
-#    - The generator guarantees several protrusions per sample, but keeps them sparse
-#      with 3x3-patch separation so the grid does not become visually noisy.
-#    - Protrusions are mostly cross-color boundary intrusions; background specks stay
-#      isolated noise. This keeps the new behavior visible without introducing too many
-#      subcases for a human solver.
-# c) Hidden assumptions in the original solver that no longer hold
-#    - Original `_clean_foreground` assumes any one-cell boundary contamination should
-#      be erased before dent detection. That assumption is invalid now.
-#    - Original output starts from the cleaned grid and only black dents become marks.
-#      In the variant, cleanup still builds the base image, but protrusion centers must
-#      be recorded before cleanup so they can be stamped afterward.
-#    - Original boundary noise generator relied on cleanup reverting inserted cells.
-#      The new generator cannot do that: inserted boundary cells are now intentional
-#      trigger points and must survive logically as protrusions.
+#    - Dents are kept in their original count range so the task is still recognizably the
+#      same family.
+#    - 4-8 protrusions are added per sample, enough to make boundary-triggered patches
+#      obvious but not enough to flood the boundary with overlapping marks.
+#    - We restrict protrusions to region-region boundaries, not arbitrary background specks.
+#      This keeps the new branch visible and avoids ambiguous patches floating in empty bg.
+#
+# c) Implicit assumptions audit
+#    - _clean_foreground() in the base task assumes any one-cell boundary anomaly should be
+#      erased. That remains useful only as a baseline reconstruction step; we therefore keep
+#      the cleanup but no longer let its edits discard information.
+#    - _dent_centers() only scans black cells, so protrusions would be invisible unless a new
+#      detector scans foreground cells that cleanup changes to the opposite foreground color.
+#    - Starting output from the cleaned grid is still valid because protrusions should not
+#      survive as raw noise; they should survive only through the stamped patch.
+#
 # d) Placement legality
-#    - Dent centers and protrusion centers are kept at Chebyshev distance >= 3, so
-#      their 3x3 patches do not overlap.
-#    - A protrusion candidate is accepted only if a local simulation shows:
-#      1. the solver classifies exactly that cell as a protrusion,
-#      2. cleaning the simulated grid reconstructs the intended clean base exactly.
-#    - Background specks are placed only on cells whose 4-neighbors are all black, so
-#      they remain removable noise rather than accidental protrusions.
+#    - Every dent/protrusion center is chosen with Chebyshev separation >= 3, so 3x3 stamps
+#      do not overlap.
+#    - A protrusion candidate is accepted only if inserting that one cell and then applying
+#      cleanup reconstructs the exact clean grid. This guarantees the solver's baseline.
+#    - Protrusions are rejected if they would form a 2x2 block of their source color, which
+#      would stop them from behaving like single-cell bulges.
+#
 # e) Visual observability
-#    - Compared with the original task, the new samples contain teal-centered 3x3
-#      patches anchored on color boundary mistakes, not just on black holes.
-#    - Because protrusions are inserted deliberately and repeatedly, the variant can be
-#      inferred from a few examples without relying on hidden metadata.
-# f) Visual neatness
-#    - The two large rectangles remain the dominant structure.
-#    - Trigger spacing prevents overlapping patch carpets, and background specks stay
-#      away from triggers so they do not obscure the intended interactions.
-# g) Color inference
